            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping &amp; thresholds along the exterior doors, and through the floor in the front bedroom for the pluming pipes. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. For the side door off the dining room, I recommend installing a proper threshold for a door sweep. 

Air leakage was observed along top plates where the walls and ceiling meet. This was very observable in the back bedroom where there is a gap in drywall by the back corner. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. I recommend checking the exhaust vent of the bathroom fan when working on the roof/attic space, as I suspect the bathroom fan may be exhausting directly into the attic space. 

The hole in the floor within the front bedroom closet had a lot of air leakage, which also may lead to frozen pipes if not looked after as it is very close to the bathroom plumbing. 



Savings: For a minimum 30% improvement you could potentially save $ 340 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, adding additional R10 exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1.5 inches of extruded polystyrene. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R10 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $ 160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double ($3,000) that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 6.0 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled increasing the attic insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $ 300 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 375 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double ($750) that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x6 joists with R20 fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3.0 inches of extruded polystyrene to the underside of the floor. By the design of the floor, I suspect there is quite a bit of heat loss through the ducting as the furnace air travels to the destinations (specifically the back bedroom, with the longest 'run'). As I suspect the underside of the ducting is minimally insulated, therefore exposed to the cold crawlspace. 



Savings:

By adding 3.0 inches (R15) inches of extruded polystyrene to the floor above your crawlspace, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.4 GJ. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double &amp; triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



This includes the patio door, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. 



Savings: 

By replacing all windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 190 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.1 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double ($3,000) that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

